### Why Does This Problem Matter?

Combinations is the fixed-size specialization of subset generation. While subsets generates all 2^n subsets of any size, combinations restricts to exactly K items. Many real-world problems ask for selections of a specific size: teams, committees, test suites, and portfolio allocations.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CombinationCount {
    // C(N, K) = N! / (K! * (N-K)!)
    public static long binomial(int n, int k) {
        if (k > n - k) k = n - k;  // optimization: C(n,k) = C(n,n-k)
        long result = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            result = result * (n - i) / (i + 1);
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("C(6,3) = " + binomial(6, 3));  // 20
        System.out.println("C(10,4) = " + binomial(10, 4));  // 210
    }
}
```

```text
C(6,3) = 20
C(10,4) = 210
```

### How Does the Pruning Optimization Work?

The pruning bound limits the loop so we never start a number too large to leave enough remaining numbers to fill the combination. If we need `k - current.size()` more slots and only `n - i + 1` numbers are available from i to N, we require `n - i + 1 >= k - current.size()`, which gives us `i <= n - (k - current.size()) + 1`.

This avoids exploring branches that can never produce a complete combination.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PruningDemo {
    static int withPruning = 0;
    static int withoutPruning = 0;

    public static void backtrackWithPruning(int n, int k, int start, List<Integer> current) {
        if (current.size() == k) { withPruning++; return; }
        for (int i = start; i <= n - (k - current.size()) + 1; i++) {
            current.add(i);
            backtrackWithPruning(n, k, i + 1, current);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1);
        }
    }

    public static void backtrackWithout(int n, int k, int start, List<Integer> current) {
        if (current.size() == k) { withoutPruning++; return; }
        for (int i = start; i <= n; i++) {
            current.add(i);
            backtrackWithout(n, k, i + 1, current);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        backtrackWithPruning(10, 3, 1, new ArrayList<>());
        backtrackWithout(10, 3, 1, new ArrayList<>());
        System.out.println("With pruning: " + withPruning + " complete combos found");
        System.out.println("Without pruning: " + withoutPruning + " complete combos found");
    }
}
```

```text
With pruning: 120 complete combos found
Without pruning: 120 complete combos found
```

Both produce the same result, but pruning avoids exploring dead-end branches, reducing the total number of recursive calls.

### How Is This Different from Permutations?

| Aspect | Combinations | Permutations |
|---|---|---|
| Order matters | No, {1,2} = {2,1} | Yes, [1,2] differs from [2,1] |
| Output count | C(N, K) | N! |
| Selection | Choose K from N | Arrange all N |
| Tracking | Start index (increasing picks) | Used array (any unused element) |
| Growth | Polynomial for fixed K | Factorial |

```java
// Combinations of 5 choose 2 = 10
// Permutations of 5 = 120
```

### What Are the Edge Cases?

The edge cases are K = 0 (one empty combination), K = N (one combination containing all numbers), and K > N (no valid combinations).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CombEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // K = 0: one empty combination
        System.out.println(CombinationGenerator.combine(5, 0));  // [[]]

        // K = N: one full combination
        System.out.println(CombinationGenerator.combine(3, 3));  // [[1, 2, 3]]

        // K > N: no valid combinations
        System.out.println(CombinationGenerator.combine(3, 5));  // []
    }
}
```

```text
[[]]
[[1, 2, 3]]
[]
```

### What About Memory and Performance?

The total number of combinations is C(N, K). For moderate N and K the count is manageable, but for large N the output can be enormous. For example, C(30, 15) is over 155 million.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CombFeasibility {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] ns = {10, 15, 20, 25, 30};
        for (int n : ns) {
            int k = n / 2;
            System.out.println("C(" + n + "," + k + ") = " + CombinationCount.binomial(n, k));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
C(10,5) = 252
C(15,7) = 6435
C(20,10) = 184756
C(25,12) = 5200300
C(30,15) = 155117520
```

### What Are Common Mistakes?

The first mistake is forgetting to pass `i + 1` instead of `i` as the next start, which would allow the same number to be picked twice. The second mistake is not pruning, which causes unnecessary recursion into branches that cannot complete.

```java
// WRONG: allows same number twice
backtrack(n, k, i, current, result);  // should be i + 1

// CORRECT: strictly increasing picks
backtrack(n, k, i + 1, current, result);
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:

- Combination Sum: choose numbers that sum to a target instead of a fixed count.
- Combination Sum III: choose exactly K numbers from 1-9 that sum to a target (combines both constraints).
- Generate the k-th combination directly using the combinatorial number system.
- Count combinations without generating them using the binomial coefficient formula.

